The connectivity described in ticket #9001 — pc1 (195.11.14.2) unable to reach pc2 (200.1.1.2) — does not exist as a fault in this network. Every layer was inspected and found fully operational.

At the data-plane layer, 10 consecutive pings from pc1 to pc2 completed with 0% packet loss and consistent sub-2 ms round-trip times. At the routing layer, both routers carry correct entries: router1 holds a BGP-learned route `B>* 200.1.1.0/24 via 193.10.11.2` and router2 holds `B>* 195.11.14.0/24 via 193.10.11.1`. At the control-plane layer, the eBGP session between router1 (AS 1, 193.10.11.1) and router2 (AS 2, 193.10.11.2) is in `Established` state, has been stable since the lab started (00:45:28 UTC on 2026-08-06), and is exchanging keepalives every 60 seconds with no resets or flaps recorded in either router's FRR log. Interface addressing, host default gateways, and IP forwarding are all correctly configured. No iptables rules exist on either router that could filter transit traffic.

The user's report of a connectivity outage is a false premise: no fault is present in the network today. The recommended action is to ask the reporter to retest from their machine and verify whether the issue may be local to their host (e.g., a stale ARP entry, local firewall, or misconfigured application proxy) rather than a network-side failure.
